<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dositej Obradović</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1739–<a href="/years/1811">1811</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dositej Obradović was a Serbian writer, translator and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> thinker who promoted writing in the vernacular Serbian language rather than Church Slavonic. After decades of travel and study across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-serbian-uprising">First Serbian Uprising</a> and became the first minister of education of Revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, helping to found the Great School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1808.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dositej-obradovic.jpg" alt="Dositej Obradović" width="308" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dositej_obradovic_Novi_Sad.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
